# cuttr project — `presentations:`, the picture moved aside and held.
#
# A screen recording shows what somebody did and not why. The usual answer is to
# cut away to a card, which loses the picture, or to put a caption over it,
# which fights it. This is the third answer: **stop the recording, move it into
# a corner, and put the point on screen beside it** — then carry on from exactly
# where it stopped.
#
#     cuttr-render examples/presentation/screencast.cuttrproj    # once, first
#     cuttr-render examples/presentation/explainer.cuttrproj
#
# The footage is made by the first of those. See `screencast.cuttrproj` for why
# this example needs a recording at all when none of the others do.
#
# ## Four things worth watching for
#
# **`at:` is on the take's clock.** The same clock the clip's own `start:` and
# `end:` are on — not the programme's. That is not a detail. A hold *changes*
# programme times, so a treatment written in them would have to know where it
# was before it could say how long the clip is. Written on the take's clock, it
# survives re-trimming the clip and moving it about the timeline.
#
# **The programme gets longer and nothing is skipped.** This clip is 24 seconds
# of footage and 39 seconds of programme: 5 + 6 + 4 seconds of holding. The
# recording picks up on the frame it stopped on. Watch the pointer.
#
# **The clip's sound stops with its picture.** A frozen picture over running
# narration reads as a dropped frame. So a hold is silent, and anything said
# over it goes on the separate recorder — which is what `audio:` in a take file
# is for, and why alignment lives there.
#
# **`bullets` and `boxes` come with the program.** They take `one:` through
# `five:`, draw only the lines given, and lay themselves out in whichever side
# of the frame the picture has left free. Define a scene of your own called
# `bullets` and yours wins — the same rule the built-in text styles follow. The
# third treatment below does exactly that.
cuttr-project: 1

takes:
  - screencast.cuttr

output:
  size: 1280x720
  fps:  25
  file: explainer.mov

timeline:
  - clip: install
    presentations:
      # Four seconds in, the first step has just appeared. The picture goes
      # left, three lines come up on the right together, and after five seconds
      # the recording carries on.
      - at:    00:04.400
        into:  [0.04, 0.2, 0.44, 0.6]
        hold:  5
        scene: bullets
        with:  {one: Grab the disk image, three: No terminal needed, two: It is notarised}

      # The second, on the other side, one line at a time. `reveal: one-by-one`
      # divides the hold evenly — three lines over six seconds is one every two
      # — so dragging the hold longer re-times them and there is nothing to
      # keep in step by hand.
      - at:     00:11.400
        into:   [0.52, 0.2, 0.44, 0.6]
        hold:   6
        scene:  boxes
        with:   {one: "Drag, do not copy", three: It replaces an older one, two: "Applications, not Desktop"}
        reveal: one-by-one

      # And the third with a scene of this project's own, named `bullets`, which
      # beats the built-in of that name. A treatment's scene is an ordinary
      # scene: it plays full frame for the hold and is authored to leave the
      # picture room.
      - at:    00:18.400
        into:  [0.04, 0.24, 0.42, 0.52]
        hold:  4
        ramp:  0.9
        scene: the-catch
        with:  {title: the first open, under: "right-click, then Open"}

scenes:
  the-catch:
    parts:
      - shape: "#f0883e"
        corner: 0.004
        keys:
          - {t: 0, x: 0.72, y: 0.62, opacity: 1, width: 0, height: 0.005}
          - {t: 0.8, width: 0.34, ease: out}
      - text:  "{{title}}"
        style: title
        keys:
          - {t: 0.3, x: 0.72, y: 0.53, opacity: 0, scale: 0.96}
          - {t: 1, opacity: 1, scale: 1, ease: out}
      - text:  "{{under}}"
        style: caption
        keys:
          - {t: 0.6, x: 0.72, y: 0.43, opacity: 0}
          - {t: 1.3, opacity: 1, ease: out}

# How the footage would be recorded for real.
#
# `File → Record Screencast…` reads this: it opens a browser of cuttr's own —
# its own profile, so none of your bookmarks, extensions or accounts are in the
# frame — at this size, showing this URL, and records that *window* rather than a
# rectangle of the screen. What lands beside this file is a `.mov` and a take for
# it. See `docs/screencasts.md`.
#
# It is written down rather than done once, so the same recording can be made
# again in a month with one line changed.
#
# `screencast.cuttrproj` beside this file draws a stand-in browser out of scene
# parts, and stays: an example in this repository has to render on a machine
# with nothing installed and no permission granted, and a real recording needs
# Chrome, a URL that still exists, and somebody at the keyboard.
recordings:
- as: install-demo
  url: https://example.com/download
